// result_end
\caption(The circular native diagram shows actual vertex IDs and color numbers. The final three probes come from the shared kernel, not a replay inferred from the final coloring.)
\alttext(Five circular nodes have colors zero, one, zero, one and two around a cycle. The last node must avoid both colors used by its two earlier neighbors.)

> Define the graph being solved

For a loopless undirected graph \(G=(V,E)\), a proper coloring maps vertices to
nonnegative integer labels with \(c(u)\ne c(v)\) on every edge. The chromatic
number \(\chi(G)\) is the smallest possible number of distinct labels. Empty
graphs have \(\chi=0\); a nonempty edgeless graph has \(\chi=1\). A bipartite
graph with at least one edge has \(\chi=2\). Without that last condition, “every
bipartite graph needs two colors” is false.

The legacy API deletes self-loops after validating their endpoints. A graph
containing a loop has no proper coloring under the mathematical definition, but
its loop-deleted projection may have one. Every properness claim below concerns
that projected graph. Parallel records remain in adjacency lists and count
towards ordering degrees. They add no new unequal-label constraint, yet can
change the heuristic's order and palette size.

The result owns one label per vertex and a contiguous palette from zero.
`is_proper_coloring` checks vector length, endpoint validity and nonloop
inequalities. It accepts arbitrary size_t labels, including gaps; it certifies
neither a claimed palette count nor minimality. Its loop-deletion policy matches
the solver, so it does not certify the original looped graph.

> Derive first fit and the degree bound

First prepare undirected adjacency lists. Sort vertices by decreasing adjacency
length, then increasing ID. For a current vertex v, initialize degree(v)+1 false
markers, scan every adjacent entry, and mark any already-colored neighbor label
that fits the marker array. Choose the smallest unmarked slot. A neighbor label
above that array cannot equal a chosen slot, so skipping such a marker is safe.

Properness follows by considering the later endpoint of each nonloop edge: it
sees the earlier endpoint's color and avoids it, either through a blocked marker
or because that label is above its possible palette. At most degree(v) distinct
colors can be blocked by its neighbors. Among degree(v)+1 slots at least one is
free, giving

$$c(v)\le\deg(v),\qquad
K\le\Delta+1\quad\text{for nonempty graphs}.$$
\alttext(The chosen zero-based label is at most the vertex degree, so the total contiguous palette contains at most the maximum degree plus one colors.)

The stronger statement replaces multiplicity degree by the number of distinct
adjacent vertices in the counting argument. Multiplicity remains relevant to the
implemented order and work. The palette is contiguous: introducing label k
requires labels 0 through k−1 already to block a vertex. A degree ceiling is an
upper bound on this feasible result, not a proof that it equals \(\chi\).

For a nonempty graph, let \(d_i\) be the degree of the i-th visited vertex,
counting positions from one. At most i−1 earlier vertices can block distinct
colors, so the degree-sequence form of the bound is

$$K\le\max_{1\le i\le n}\min(d_i+1,i)\le\Delta+1.$$
\alttext(The palette is bounded by the maximum, over visit positions, of the smaller of degree plus one and the one-based position.)

This upper bound still need not be sharp: a regular crown gives the same
degree-sequence ceiling under either numbering, while the actual palettes can
differ.

The five-cycle needs three colors because alternating two labels around an odd
cycle clashes at closure. The clique \(K_4\) needs four because every vertex
pair conflicts. These lower bounds, paired with the native feasible colorings,
establish optimality for these particular examples.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/coloring_clique.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto g = dm::algorithms::coloring_complete(4);
    const auto a = dm::algorithms::greedy_coloring_analyze(g.vertices, g.edges);
    std::print("| vertex | degree | actual color |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto v : a.order)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", v, a.degree[v], a.result.color[v]);
    std::print("\nK4 uses {}; maximum degree plus one {}; proper {}; each pair is adjacent, so four is also " "a lower bound.\n",
               a.result.num_colors, a.work.max_degree + 1,
               dm::algorithms::is_proper_coloring(g.vertices, g.edges,
                                                  a.result.color));
    dm::graphics::Diagram d =
        dm::algorithms::coloring_diagram(g, a.result,
                                         "K4: four mutually conflicting vertices");
    d.save_svg("build/coloring_clique.svg");
    if (a.result.num_colors != 4)
        throw std::runtime_error("original clique");
}
