> A bipartite graph where tie order wastes colors

Take two sides \(A=\{a_0,\ldots,a_{k-1}\}\) and \(B=\{b_0,\ldots,b_{k-1}\}\).
Join \(a_i\) to every \(b_j\) except \(b_i\). This crown graph has 2k vertices,
k(k−1) edges and degree k−1 everywhere. For k≥2 it has an edge and an explicit
proper two-coloring: all A vertices zero, all B vertices one. Thus \(\chi=2\).

Give IDs in paired order \(a_0,b_0,a_1,b_1,\ldots\). Degree ties retain this
sequence. Inductively, both vertices of pair i see colors 0 through i−1 on
previous opposite-side vertices, but they do not conflict with one another. Both
take color i. The native palette therefore has k colors, matching the degree
ceiling while wasting a factor k/2 over the optimum.

Grouped IDs \(a_0,\ldots,a_{k-1},b_0,\ldots,b_{k-1}\) give the same abstract
graph a different tie order. First fit then uses two colors. This is a
relabeling effect, not a new optimization algorithm or an empirical average-case
result.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/coloring_crown.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto g = dm::algorithms::coloring_crown(4, true);
    const auto a = dm::algorithms::greedy_coloring_analyze(g.vertices, g.edges);
    const auto witness = dm::algorithms::coloring_crown_witness(4, true);
    std::print("| vertex | greedy label | explicit bipartition label |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t v = 0; v < g.vertices; ++v)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", v, a.result.color[v], witness.color[v]);
    const auto grouped = dm::algorithms::coloring_crown(4, false);
    const auto other =
        dm::algorithms::greedy_coloring(grouped.vertices, grouped.edges);
    const bool feasible =
        dm::algorithms::is_proper_coloring(g.vertices, g.edges, witness.color);
    std::print("\nPaired-ID greedy {}; grouped-ID greedy {}; two-color witness proper {}; nonempty " "bipartite graph has exact optimum 2.\n",
               a.result.num_colors, other.num_colors, feasible);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d =
        dm::algorithms::coloring_diagram(g, a.result,
                                         "Crown with paired IDs: greedy spends four colors");
    d.text({0, -4.7}, "Even IDs form side A; odd IDs form side B", 22).save_svg(
        "build/coloring_crown.svg");
    if (a.result.num_colors != 4 || other.num_colors != 2 || !feasible)
        throw std::runtime_error("crown witness");
}
