\image(figures/coloring_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The two repeated records change degree ordering without changing the underlying simple graph. The loop result concerns only the loop-deleted projection; no proper coloring exists for the original looped graph.)
\alttext(Five bars show palette sizes three, two, three, zero and one for the base crown, repeated-edge crown, loop-added crown, empty graph and three isolates.)

The analysis APIs cap complete vertices and original edge records at one million
each by default, including isolates, loops and duplicates; zero disables either
cap. Full endpoint validation precedes those caps and adjacency allocation. The
legacy API has no optional policy caps. Capture additionally admits each actual
whole event online, default one million; zero disables that cap. Preparation,
ordinary result/work, capacities and allocation overhead are excluded, so these
are neither byte nor peak-memory limits. Exceeding a cap throws without
returning a partial trace. Analysis without capture ignores the event cap.

Owned preparation, result labels and events survive input mutation and
independent copies. The verifier returns false on wrong shape or invalid
endpoints; it does not throw for an ordinary invalid candidate. Independent
calls share no mutable state, while concurrent mutation of borrowed input
remains outside the contract.

> What the examples support

Properness is a feasibility guarantee for a stated graph model. Optimality
additionally needs a lower bound matching the palette, as with an odd cycle,
clique or explicit two-color crown witness. Degree ordering is deterministic and
cheap, but even regular bipartite graphs expose unbounded waste from ID ties.
Parallel records affect both selected work and ordering. Synthetic graphs are
appropriate here because they isolate these mechanisms; no unrelated dataset or
real scheduling-performance claim is needed.

D. J. A. Welsh and M. B. Powell, [An upper bound for the chromatic number of a
graph and its application to timetabling
problems](https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/10.1.85), The Computer Journal 10(1),
85–86 (1967), is the primary source for degree-ordered coloring and its
scheduling motivation. Ascending-ID ties, preserved parallel entries, loop
deletion and the event format are this library's explicit software policy.
Scheduling applications need their own conflict model; an undirected edge does
not encode capacities, durations or every real timetable constraint.

Verification compares all six-vertex simple graphs and seeded multigraphs with a
frozen original implementation, reconstructs actual events and work, and uses
independent exact-color searches on small graphs. Quota, malformed-endpoint,
loop, empty/isolate, ownership and family-size cases run with sanitizers. All
complete programs, figures, documentation examples and both exports are checked
before the legacy sources are retired.
